# Review dates and freshness

The NHS review-date component has been withdrawn. Use its replacement content-review pattern, and keep that distinct from sensor freshness.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## Use the maintained editorial-date family

Use [Page metadata and history](page-history.html#own-editorial-dates-without-inventing-freshness) for the maintained publication and update component, and [Show when guidance was reviewed](../patterns/review-dates.html) for the review workflow. This URL remains the withdrawn NHS source reference with its historical examples. Keeping it does not reinstate the retired upstream component or authorise automatic freshness dates.

## When to use it

Show a review date on authored guidance when it helps people judge whether the advice is maintained. Record the actual editorial review and the intended next review, rather than changing the date on every deployment.

## When not to use it

Do not put editorial review metadata inside a transaction, or reset a whole-page review date after changing only one sentence. Place whole-page review dates after the authored content. Do not label a sensor value “reviewed”. A data timestamp identifies when a value was observed or received; it does not show that a person checked its accuracy.

## Home Assistant adaptation

For civica.uk documentation, published and updated metadata describe changes to the page. A device card instead needs the meaning of its timestamp, the relevant timezone and an unavailable or stale state when required by its integration. Keep these concepts separate in wording and machine-readable records.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use visible text with semantic time elements and a full unambiguous date. Relative text such as “five minutes ago” can supplement an absolute timestamp but should not be the only information.

